A||||36||FIEROCIOUS||EGR  removal=meltdown WHY?||||5||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||02-09-2000||01:41 AM||||I've heard correct me if I'm wrong, that if you remove your EGR valve that you will melt your pistons. I don't remember which pistons melt (feel free to answer) Ed Parks site says they will melt "He's seen many." I've known people to run without the EGR in their Fiero (No problems yet) And I've built many engines without EGRs: SB Chevys, Ford Winsors, ect. No problems. <BR>In theory the EGR lets Exhaust gasses enter the combustion chamber cooling the combustion process by inert (non explosive) gases causeing a less volitile mixture. Why does the fiero melt? Poor cast pistons? Poor head design? I've been asked this a million times and have yet to find th TRUE answer! ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||02-09-2000||02:03 AM||||The egr is designed to cool the combustion chamber.  From what I've learned, modern fuel-injected engines run as lean a mix as possible, which in turn makes the chamber hotter.  The egr is one way to keep temps down.  <P>I guess it's more of a hit or miss whether your engine will melt a piston or not.  Well, the piston doesn't actually melt, a hole is burned through it.  the #2 piston is the most common on to get a hole, with the #5 next.  <P>The way I see it is, the engine was <B>designed</B> to have an operating egr.  There is nothing wrong with the pistons, nothing wrong with the head.  I suppose if you're determined to run without an egr, you could figure out a way to make the f/a mix a little richer to lower combustion temps.. but you'll have a hard time getting through a smog check.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||02-09-2000||02:17 AM||||Adjustable fuel pressure regulator and a Auto meter A/F gage already reside in my car all I have to do to change the ratio is a twist of a screw! Reason I ask is I race and Simple stupid has always won me races if I could take the EGR and the solinoid off less stuff to go wrong! <BR>P.S. bypass the wireing no code 32 ect.<BR>"Not that I have this problem!"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||02-09-2000||04:02 AM||||I've never heard of a measurable performance increase from the egr being disconnected, or connected, for that matter.  Also, the egr is only used at part throttle/low load conditions, such as when you're cruising down the street.  It is closed at idle and at full throttle.  I've heard about the wiring bypass before.  <BR>I don't believe that the egr going out would cause you to lose a race... if the engineers who designed and built the engine didn't see a need for it, it wouldn't be there.<P>Can you really adjust the a/f mixture by raising the fuel pressure?  The injectors would spray more fuel at each pulse, but the ecm would see this and compensate for it.  the ecm strives to stay as close to the stochiometric whatever as it can.  changing one variable too much can throw the ecm out of wack and result in less performance.  i think.  i may be talking outta my @$$ here.  please correct me if i'm wrong.||||reg||4
Z||000004||DRH||02-09-2000||01:36 PM||||Standard is right on target with everything I have heard on the subject. The timing and injector pulse width calculations in the ECM are counting on a working EGR. <P>The link below goes into more explanation. <P>Note: I have no idea if the person that wrote this knew their stuff or not... BUT it does make sense and it is consistent with other info I have seen from respected sources.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/EGR.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/EGR.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||kidzwheelz||02-09-2000||02:21 PM||||  Fierocious, With the way you drive that poor car, it's going to melt down eventually anyway. Why don't you just take it off and be our guinea pig? I think after you slammed that car, it may be the only thing around that is lower than my truck, without hydraulics or air bags!||||reg||10
Z||000006||JIM GOOSE||02-09-2000||02:30 PM||||You can put a BB in the vac-line where it connects to the valve and it will look stock but won't function (open).  My car has been this way for some time and no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Capt Fiero||02-09-2000||02:55 PM||||My EGR pipe cracked about 2 years ago, so I removed it, pluged both ends so I would not get a vacumme leak into the intake and so that I would not get an exhaust leak at the pod end.  I needed to leave the hose connected and the pipe in place due to very strict emmissions testing, yes they look under the deck to make sure that you have all of your stuff they just don't check to see if it is working or not.  I have been running very hard every day. I run 94oct gas here in Canada you can get 94 at any Chevron or Mohawk station.  I recently did a compression test, all cyl read even at 145 within 6%.  So I guess I do not have a bad cyl yet.  I am planning on installing a new motor at some point soon, just because I am getting tired of the 2.8 would like to get a 3.4 in good shape. Could be fun, then who cares about the EGR. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||FIEROCIOUS||02-09-2000||03:19 PM||||The biggest problem with the EGR removal is that when you want to do it people ask WHY? Well I more than likely won't "howerver," If you did why does it melt and other engines do not? That's the real question! YES I understand that the EGR only works at part throttle and under certain load conditions.YES it will not loose me a race "but thats a good excuse LOL" The SPEED DENSITY program our computer uses is not very accurate. Also for those useing the 160 degree themostat the computer doesn't control the fuel metering via O2 sensor until 180 degrees.<BR>Does the fuel pressure regulator work? <BR>YES. <BR>How do you know? <BR>I have one on the Civic I own and the Fiero. It won't blow your mind the amount of power differance it gives but it does balance the system, especialy if you have air inhancement mods! Most systems don't run enough fuel pressure. The computer does compensate for the added pressure, well if it did than WHY DOESN'T IT For the LEAN condition caused by no EGR???? Now I got you all think'n don't I? <BR>The A/F gauge cycles back and fourth as the computer incressess and decresses the ratio when you raise the pressure it cycles at a RICHER fuel mixture! (That means it works) Some considerations about raising the pressure:<BR>1. The fuel patern gets stronger and finer, this makes for better mixing of the fuel and air!<BR>2. The speed in which the fuel comes out is faster this lowers the temp of the combustion chamber, this is caused both by the faster spray but also by the evaporation caused by the finer mist!<BR>Now as for the computer working for staying stochiometric this is false (just a bit leaner)<BR>The computer has no metering devices at full throttle so the increase in fuel goes unhindered! but runs fuel eficient at normal conditions. Good gas miledge and full throttle power!<BR>Why take it off?<BR>Cracked EGR tube. (it's 90.00)<BR>Turbo install. (no room)<BR>Less mess in the engine compartment.<BR>Cleaner intake track. (no carbon build up)<BR>JUST TO NAME A FEW!<BR>Thanks for any info you can correct me on!<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eddie.H||02-09-2000||05:47 PM||||"O" "O" WILL thats it now you did it some call Theogre lets go get Theogre.||||reg||10
Z||000010||FIEROCIOUS||02-10-2000||12:41 AM||||Theogre any thoughts? ||||reg||5
Z||000011||Standard||02-10-2000||12:59 AM||||Removing the EGR doesn't cause a lean condition, it just makes the chamber hotter.  The egr is in no way connected with fuel delivery.  I was just pointing out that if you took it off you might want to run a richer mix to keep temps down.<P>I know the computer defaults to a slightly rich mixture at ful throttle, and doesn't use the input from any sensors, but at anything less it'll try to stay stoch, sfaik.  I believe most people know that if you drop in a 160 thermostat you need to use an aftermarket chip, and should also have a 176 fan switch.<P>Like I said above, i think it's pretty much a hit or miss whether or not you're going to get a hole.  You may get lucky and have absolutly no problems, or you might not be so lucky.  I need to get my EGR tube fixed, too, so I'm in the same boat as you.  You don't have to buy a new one, tho.  You can get the old one welded, or go junkyard scrounging and pull one off of a firebird w/ a 2.8l V6.  all you have to do is slot the holes on the intake side, and it'll work fine.<P>Ogre, where are you?<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 02-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||FIEROCIOUS||02-10-2000||02:49 AM||||Stochiometric is the perfect combustion ratio, if you live in a perfect world. <BR>I don't need the EGR tube I guess I'm just trying to be a pain. LOL<BR>Unexplained things bother me this is one of those things. <BR>We have determined that it does not lean out with the EGR off, but the temperature of the #2 and #5 should not get much hotter than the other cylinders (even if they are the middle cylinders) not hot enough to put a hole in the cylinder!!!<BR>I'm just trying to find if this is anouther Fiero urban tail! Don't you know all fieros spontaniously combust!<BR>I have read past threads on this and feel it was dismissed because people listened to the WHY TAKE IT OFF argument but failed to figure out why it frys the engine!<BR>Also Standard if the factory had a choice it wouldn't be there! The EGR is on all engines after 75 because of THE MAN! remember the A.I.R. pumps, worthless, now that emissions can be kept low enough without the use of it manufacturers don't use it! What about thermo switches? don't see many of those either. but at one time they were mandatory!<P>The EGR allows recirculation of a small amount of exhaust gas back into the intake manifold. This lowers the amount of nitrous oxide emissions from the engine.<BR>With the new three way catalytic converters being so efficient at removeing NoX emmisions why run a EGR?<BR>I agree it is hit and miss.<BR>WHY?<BR>That is my question!<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||la9||02-10-2000||01:16 PM||||Don't know if this will help anyone but I just used an EGR tube from a 1986 pontiac 6000 2.8L. You just need to slot the one end to make it fit.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Standard||02-10-2000||01:58 PM||||I never knew egr had anything to do with fuel flow.. thanks for settin' me straight, terry.. now I should really get mine fixed.  ||||reg||14
Z||000016||theogre||02-10-2000||02:27 PM||||here I is,,,,,,,,<P>I've run things with and w/o EGR over the years..... On all my ford 4 cyls I eventually blocked it off. They leaked, the car passed emmisions w/o it, so I just blocked it. My 6 & 8 cyl fords didn't have EGR,,, too old.<P>On my 87 DIS motor if the EGR isn't working it doesn't like it. I haven't really looked at all of why.<P>I've heard of problems removing computer controlled EGR on some motors. I suspect it's partly because these motor may also have EGR feedback to the ECM.<P>A EGR that's working properly doesn't cause a major change to the mix. It should thin the mix but not change the F/A ratio. At least it shouldn't if the thing isn't already rich or lean in what case the unused fuel/air in the exhaust will also be sampled into the intake by the EGR.<P>Again allot of it depends on the motor in question. I've never seen a motor burn a piston unless it had some other problem. Most of the EGR's I've seen on cars thru the late 80's don't work anyway because the intake manifold port is with carbon, the EGR tubing is plugged, or the EGR valve is bad or plugged. (My ford 4 cyl passed emmisions for years with the EGR blocked off. I had over 190,000 miles on it.)<P>As long as the EGR is working I'd keep it on. It does help reduce emmisions under certain load conditions. I have mine in working order. I disabled it once while checking for another problem and it actually seemed to run worse. But then mine is a DIS motor which is in it's own little world.||||reg||1
Z||000017||FIEROCIOUS||02-10-2000||02:43 PM||||Standard, DRH gave a link to the fiero online service guide that goes into detail about the computer and how the pulse width and advance change with the EGR amount at X amount of load.<BR>Randy T Agee nows his stuff!<BR>I've talked to him several times.<BR>However, the ECM uses the EGR solinoid as a means to control the Exhaust gas flow into the intake this is all controled by  map as seen on DRH link this map can be altered by a prom burning company (ADS will do it for 25.00 after you own one of their chips.)<BR>ADS has several companys around the US that can perform this operation. This is the same charge if you say changed from a 2.8 to a 3.4! <BR>Has anyone had a motor melt?<BR>P.S. my EGR is in perfect working order, people imply the oposite.<BR>||||reg||1
