Z||000019||FIEROCIOUS||02-10-2000||06:48 PM||||Kidzwheelz thanks for putting things back on track. I forgot about my original question with all this techno talk.<P>Why do the #2 and #5 pistons melt after you take the EGR off? I still can't find a reason even after all the good input to the tread, Thanks to all who contributed!<BR>IF YOU MADE IT THIS FAR DOWN THE THREAD YOU KNOW MORE THAN YOU EVER WANTED TO KNOW ABOUT EGR's.<BR>Buffalo nickle to the first person who answers why they melt!||||reg||1
Z||000020||lowCG||02-10-2000||07:10 PM||||I believe that the hot exhaust gasses being introduced to the intake make for an effectively richer mix;take away the hot gasses,and the cooler mixture wants to burn faster,detonating.On cars without a functioning egr,that were intended to have one,should have some slight form of enrichment,that hopefully won't keep the motor from leaning out properly at the top end on the race track. ||||reg||1
