Z||000022||Standard||02-11-2000||12:33 AM||||I don't know why they melt, but 2 and 5 are not the center pistons.  the pistons are numbered like this:<P>driver  6 4 2  passenger<BR>side    5 3 1  side<BR>        trunk<BR>something to do with the firing order?  The way the intake is set up?  The way the coolant passages are set up in the block?  anybody?<P>the diagram doesn't quite come out right.. click edit if you wanna see how it should be..<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 02-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||theogre||02-11-2000||09:30 AM||||With inline V motors it's usually the back pistons/cylinders that cook. Those cyl's don't get the cooling the front ones do. Could be a similar coolant flow problem in Fiero V6. Could be something as simple as a head gasket needing a small change. (Presence and size of coolant openings in the head gasket are commonly used to meter coolant flow. That's one of the reasons they have to be installed exactly the way they where made to go even if they may physically fit in more than one way. I don't know about Fiero, but some head gaskets can be installed backwards.)<P>The Coolant in the 4 cyl goes in the front and out the back so 3 & 4 run somewhat hotter than 1 & 2. This motor is smaller so it's not a huge issue but it still happens.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||FIEROCIOUS||02-11-2000||03:41 PM||||Standard your correct on the cylinder numbers<BR>(I was momentarly retarded)<BR>I had a picture of the cylinders in my head but common sense didn't intervein!<BR>It made more sense when I was thinking the center two. Chevy 400's had a problem with the center cylinders, I think ogre has something with the head gasket.<BR>Chevrolet Power- "Modify the production head gasket by enlargeing the small water passage holes for the end cylinders to 9/32-inch diameter. Enlarge the two center water holes to 1/4-inch." sounds like a posibility if the factory raceing effort saw this as nessasary.<BR>We are close but I'm STILL not satisfied. <BR>What is next?||||reg||1
