Z||000029||88formula||02-16-2000||06:15 PM||||The sole reasons why a disconnected EGR can melt a piston is because of detonation, pre-ignition, and elevated combusion temperatures. When you disconnect an EGR valve, which cools the combustion gases, the combustion temperatures elevate which makes you more susceptible to detonation, pre-ignition and burnt pistons. Detonation and pre-ignition tend to burn holes through the ring lands like a blow torch, but what happens most of the time is not burnt pistons, but broken ring lands.  The erratic explosions of fuel from these two slam the pistons like a hammer, this pounding of the pistons is the be be sound you hear when detonation occurs. Older engines ran with richer fuel mixtures which cool the combustion temperatures and thus mitigate the above mentioned<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 02-16-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000030||DRH||02-16-2000||06:26 PM||||Somewhere way back up there it was mentioned that the ECM advanced the timing when it called for EGR. The thought being the EGR would make up for the advanced timing. So what problem can advancing timing too far cause??? <B>Detonation</B><P>Also, someone mentioned running high octane. The 2.8 is designed to run on plain old 87 octane. Maybe running high octane keeps it from detonating even with the EGR disconnected???<P>Makes sense to me anyway...||||reg||3
Z||000031||theogre||02-16-2000||07:00 PM||||I ment inline mounted for RWD.<P>And yes. older 400's had heat problems. even allot of stock ones probably could have used that gasket modification.||||reg||1
Z||000032||FIEROCIOUS||02-17-2000||04:07 AM||||Ogre, the gasket modification is for the 2.8!<P>Detination is easily understood (thanks) but, I'm less concerned with general causes of piston failure and more concerned with Fiero #2 and #5 piston failure. <BR>I've looked at EGR systems and fully know how they work.<BR>I know about detination and advance and computer systems (READ ABOVE!)<BR>What I am trying to find is why the FIERO ENGINE is differant than ALL other engines in this respect!<BR>I feel that when you compare the Fiero V6 to other engines it's like an apples to oranges thing! <BR>I know the causes of piston failure, read ALL of the exellent information from all that contributed, even the information  provided on the link then put it all together, deside which of the variables are present, and you will notice that the conditions required to melt a piston realy aren't there!<BR>If a EGR is connected but pluged with carbon why is there no record of this causeing failure?<BR>Anyone fry an engine to discover a pluged EGR?  ||||reg||1
Z||000033||Standard||02-17-2000||04:49 AM||||The Fiero V6 is the standard Chevy 60* V6, mass produced and used in a bazillion cars during the 80's.  The only real difference is the plenum and intake runners, and Pontiac was the first to put MPFI on it in '85.  <P>I think the only reason that it seems like the Fiero fry's pistons without an egr is because they seem to have tube's that crack alot more easily than other versions of this engine.  I'm sure other 2.8's have this problem, but have you asked anybody with a 2.8 firebird about it?  ||||reg||1
Z||000034||terryk||02-17-2000||11:44 AM||||Both Chevy and Pontiac release the 660 MPFI engine in 85. The Chevy is the W/S engine (FWD/RWD) and the Fiero 9 engine.<P>They are the same engine other than the TB (Holley/Rochester) and mass air flow vs speed density. Yes, the upper plenums are different since the Fiero intake is on the distributor side vs the W/S being on the pulley side.<P>They both went through federal certification together as backup's to each other. Had one failed, the other would have been used. Both passed and Pontiac opted for the cheaper (cost wise) system. Camaro's and the X bodies got the S and W engines.||||reg||1
Z||000035||FIEROCIOUS||02-18-2000||04:27 AM||||Standard, great pics nice to have! It would be nice to get some pics of a fried 2.8 from a Fiero. Thanks for your help on trying to solve the mystery!<P>Terryk they actualy made a 2.8 with mass air?<BR>I didn't know that. What years and makes did it come in?<P>Sorry I've been so persistent but I STILL am not satisfied with the solutions so far.<P>So far: By removeing the EGR you take inert gasses from the intake chamber that the computer expects.<P>The computer thinks that the exhaust is recirculateing so it bumps up the timeing.<P>However there is no inert gas present so the fuel is burned prematurely.<P>This causes pressure spikes, which will damage ring lands and flaten bearings, not burn pistons! But, by not haveing the exhaust gas to cool the chamber, piston chambers become hot this would lead to burnt pistons. Allthough, only at high load situations (towing or climbing a hill) the EGR does not operate at high load! Only light part throttle situations.<P>The coolant flow goes into the front cylinder head and exits out the rear cylinder head it first goes to the #2 piston then #4 then #6 onto the otherside to #5 then to #3 and finaly #1. The coolant flow does not support the over heating theory so I will exclude the coolant system as a contributer.<P>There is a second hole in the exhaust port on the middle two cylinders to heat the intake. This is were a normal engines EGR is introduced! Not from the exhaust pipe. This may go somewere! Any ideas on this?<P>The exhaust gas is introduced just after the throttle body, it should therefore be distibuted evenly! The #2 and #5 should be at no greater risk than any of the other pistons. Right?<P>The Fuel metering is changed with the EGR open signal to lean out, causeing higher piston chamber temperatures. But the #2 and #5 only condition suggest that the injectors are controlled individualy and not as a group!<P>Thats how it doesn't add up!<BR>Did I  miss or forget something?<BR>Help me out! <BR>Thanks for all of your help and for furthering the knowledge of Fiero owners world wide!||||reg||1
Z||000036||terryk||02-18-2000||09:57 AM||||My final post on this. I've never seen a 660 wreck a piston from pinging. I've personally wrecked plugs. <P>The mass air flow on the iron head engine was used into 88 on the Citation (in 85), Celebrity, 6000, and Ciera. In 85, they used the 6870 ECM which is the same hardware as the 6869 and 7170, but different embedded code in the processor. Some parts of the board aren't loaded depending on the application. I think the 7703 was used in 86. Not sure about 87/88. The wiring between them is close enough that just some minor swaps and the addition of the mass and you can run the mass system.<P>The the W/S and 9 engines are all batch fire.<P>Ask the Buick Turbo and Supercharged folks about damage.||||reg||7
Z||000037||FIEROCIOUS||02-20-2000||02:34 AM||||Thanks again Terryk for your help and contributions!||||reg||1
